 This method implements direct volume rendering using 2D textures containing tiles that represent low-resolution 2D slices of the volume, referred to as 'pseudo volume textures'. It solves the problem of rendering dynamic volumetric data by allowing the volume to be "painted" at runtime. It differs from true 3D volume textures by restricting effective maximum volume resolution further, but distinguishes itself from its parent [Ray-marched Volumetric Lighting with Density Thresholding](2456-ray-marched-volumetric-lighting-with-density-thresholding.md) by recomputing lighting for each frame via secondary ray marches for every voxel sample.
 
+### [Light Propagation Volume (LPV) / Opacity Propagation Volume (OPV)](11130-light-propagation-volume-lpv-opacity-propagation-volume-opv.md)
+Light Propagation Volume (LPV), specifically the Opacity Propagation Volume (OPV) variant, is a real-time method for precomputing the optical depth $T(S,X)$ from each light source $S$ to each cell $X$ in a 3D grid. It solves the problem of efficiently calculating light attenuation (extinction) through heterogeneous participating media by propagating density information radially from light sources through a 3D volume on the GPU. Unlike standard radiance propagation which suffers from rapid scattering errors in dense media, OPV focuses on accurate optical depth integration, enabling correct shadowing and attenuation for subsequent ray-marched rendering. It builds on [Ray-marched Volumetric Lighting with Density Thresholding](2456-ray-marched-volumetric-lighting-with-density-thresholding.md) by providing the precomputed attenuation data required for that rendering step.
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot S)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels in the light map volume and $S$ is the number of samples per ray (`light_sample_count`).
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the output light map texture, plus input textures for density, mask, and noise.
-*   **Performance:** The method is implemented as a GPU compute shader, allowing for parallel processing of voxels. Performance depends on the resolution of the light map and the number of samples.
-*   **Strengths:** Precomputes lighting for efficient runtime rendering; handles volumetric shadows and attenuation; supports god rays effects.
-*   **Limitations:** Precomputation may not adapt well to dynamic light sources or moving volumes without recomputation; limited by the `R8` output format precision.
-*   **When to Use:** Suitable for static or slowly changing volumetric scenes where lighting precomputation is feasible.
-*   **When Not to Use:** Unsuitable for dynamic light sources or rapidly changing volumes where real-time recomputation is required.
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+The time complexity is proportional to the number of voxels in the light map volume multiplied by the number of samples per ray (`light_sample_count`). The memory complexity is proportional to the size of the 3D light map texture (`light_map_resolution.x * light_map_resolution.y * light_map_resolution.z` bytes, assuming `R8` format) plus the mask and noise textures [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+Not given in the sources.
+
+**Scaling:**
+The method scales with the resolution of the light map volume and the number of samples. Higher resolutions and sample counts increase accuracy but also increase computation time and memory usage [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+Strengths include efficient precomputation of volumetric lighting and shadows, suitable for static or slowly changing volumes. Limitations include the static nature of the light map, which may not adapt well to dynamic lighting or rapidly changing volume densities without recomputation [S1, unofficial].
+
+**When to Use:**
+Use this method when rendering static or slowly changing volumetric scenes with a global light source, where precomputed lighting is acceptable and dynamic lighting is not required [S1, unofficial].
+
+**When Not to Use:**
+Do not use this method for dynamic volumetric scenes with changing light sources or rapidly changing densities, as the precomputed light map will become outdated [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
